Heat Exhaustion
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Distributive / Insensible Loss
Profuse sweating, Dizziness/orthostatic symptoms, Intact central nervous system function

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-019
Reason for Visit: Severe fatigue, profound dizziness upon standing, and intense muscle cramping following prolonged outdoor physical work, requiring urgent evaluation for severe volume depletion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe orthostatic dizziness and an inability to maintain oral fluid intake due to nausea after working in high heat. This mandated an urgent assessment to confirm intact neurological function (excluding heat stroke) and to administer targeted oral or anti-emetic therapy to safely resolve the severe volume depletion prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Extreme weakness, dizziness, and painful muscle cramps. Patient states, I was working on the roof all afternoon, and now I feel completely drained, sick to my stomach, and I can't stand up without the room spinning.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms developed gradually over the past 6 hours after performing heavy manual labor outdoors in 34C heat. The patient reports profuse, sustained sweating throughout the day, which has saturated clothing multiple times. There is intense, unquenchable thirst. Over the last two hours, the patient has experienced extreme fatigue, weakness, and generalized malaise. Upon returning home, the patient experienced severe dizziness, lightheadedness, and a near-syncopal episode upon attempting to stand. Intense, painful muscle cramps have developed in the heavily exercised calf and thigh muscles. The patient reports moderate nausea without active vomiting or marked abdominal pain.
Functional Status: Resting on the floor; completely unable to mobilize safely due to profound orthostatic instability and cramping.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of syncope-related injury if mobilization is attempted; spouse is present and highly supportive.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 mmHg (Supine) -> 85/55 mmHg (Standing) | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 38.2 C (Tympanic) | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82.0 Kg
General: Patient is conscious, alert, visibly exhausted, and currently lying supine with legs elevated by the spouse.
Physical Exam: Neurological: The diagnostic triad is firmly established: the central nervous system function remains completely intact with a Glasgow Coma Scale of 15, normal mentation, and no confusion. Cardiovascular: Tachycardia out of proportion to current rest. Profound orthostatic hypotension is objectively confirmed upon assisted standing, reproducing severe dizziness. Integumentary: Skin is pale, cool, and clammy, with profuse sweating still actively occurring, indicating intact thermoregulatory mechanisms. Musculoskeletal: Palpable spasms noted in bilateral gastrocnemius muscles. Core body temperature is elevated (38.2C) but remains strictly below 40C (104F).
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting in a cool, well-ventilated, air-conditioned living room.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has no history of diuretic or beta-blocker use that would impair cardiovascular compensation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Heat Exhaustion - Status: Acute, stable with intact mentation
Differentials:

Heat Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Completely normal mentation, intact sweating mechanism (clammy, profuse diaphoresis), and a core body temperature remaining well below the critical 40C threshold.

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear environmental etiology for the extreme diaphoresis and tachycardia, accompanied by profound orthostatic hypotension rather than central crushing chest pain.

Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: History of extreme heat exposure and insensible fluid loss (sweat), rather than voluminous diarrhea and primary febrile illness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a classic presentation of Heat Exhaustion, characterized by massive sensible fluid and sodium loss through profuse sweating. The resulting severe intravascular volume depletion is evidenced by marked orthostatic hypotension, tachycardia, and intense thirst. Critically, the patient's mentation remains normal, strictly differentiating this from life-threatening heat stroke. Delaying assessment until standard in-hours would have left the patient at severe risk of a syncopal fall or progressive deterioration if nausea prevented oral intake. Urgent after-hours assessment confirmed the hemodynamic stability, excluded neurological involvement, and allowed for the safe initiation of a structured, high-sodium oral rehydration strategy to correct the hypovolemia. The expected investigation results include normal or mildly elevated hematocrit and normal to mildly deranged serum sodium and BUN due to volume depletion that would assist in confirming the diagnosis, though bloodwork is not immediately necessary.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ondansetron 4mg sublingually to rapidly control nausea and facilitate oral intake. Instructed the spouse to initiate aggressive oral rehydration using a commercial electrolyte solution, specifically avoiding plain free water to prevent dilutional hyponatremia and worsening cramps.
Positive Behavioral Change: To efficiently restore your blood pressure and stop the cramping, commit to drinking 250ml of the icy electrolyte solution every 15 minutes. Use a timer. When you finish a glass without feeling nauseous, consciously recognize that the medication is working and your volume is returning. This direct relief of your intense thirst and nausea will reward your strict adherence to the timed drinking schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 48 hours for a general review and blood pressure check.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the nearest Emergency Department immediately or call 000 if you become confused, if you stop sweating completely and your skin becomes hot and dry, or if you cannot keep any fluids down for more than 2 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis assumes the condition will reverse entirely with oral hydration; rapid deterioration requires reassessment.
Clinical Course: You have sweat out a massive amount of salt and water, dropping your blood pressure. With rest in a cool environment and aggressive, steady consumption of electrolyte fluids, your symptoms will resolve over the next 12 to 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not drink large quantities of plain water rapidly, as this will dangerously dilute your blood sodium and make the muscle cramps significantly worse. Only drink the electrolyte solutions provided.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sweating removed the critical volume from your blood vessels. The sublingual tablet stops your stomach from rejecting fluids, allowing us to slowly refill your blood vessels with salt-heavy fluids.
Actionable Steps: Remain lying flat. Drink 250ml of electrolyte fluid every 15 minutes. Stand up extremely slowly over the next few hours.

